s (lint, format, type-check, build, tests) before submitting changes.\n- Never claim checks passed unless they were actually run.\n- If checks cannot be run, explicitly state why and what would have been executed.\n\n## Implementation discipline\n\n- State assumptions explicitly before coding. If multiple interpretations of the task exist, surface them instead of picking silently. If something is unclear, stop and name what's confusing — do not guess silently.\n- Write the minimum code that solves the problem. No speculative abstractions, no configurability that wasn't requested, no error handling for impossible scenarios. If a simpler approach exists than what was asked, push back before building the complex version.\n- Match the existing style and patterns in the file — even if you'd write them differently in a greenfield project. Raise the disagreement separately if it matters.\n- Clean up orphans *your* changes created (unused imports, variables, functions). Do not delete pre-existing dead code unless asked — flag it instead.\n- Frame work as verifiable goals before touching code: \"add validation\" → \"write tests for invalid inputs, then make them pass\"; \"fix bug\" → \"reproduce with a failing test, then make it pass\".\n- For multi-step work, write a brief plan with a per-step verification check before executing, so you can loop independently instead of asking for confirmation each step.\n- Every changed line should trace back to the task. If it doesn't, drop it.\n\n## CLI tools\n\nPrefer the Nix-managed tools below over slower or less precise defaults when they fit the task.\n\n| tool             | replaces                        | usage                                                                               |\n|------------------|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `rg` (`ripgrep`) | `grep`                          | `rg \"pattern\"` - fast recursive text search 